WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.176
It's eight in the morning at a busy hospital.

00:00:03.176 --> 00:00:06.380
Phones are ringing, the waiting room is filling up,

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:10.462
and on the wards, the night team is handing over to the day team.

00:00:11.962 --> 00:00:18.560
This is Setu26: one platform that connects a hospital, its staff and its patients.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:25.534
Let's follow one family through a hospital stay, from the first phone call to the day they go home.

00:00:27.034 --> 00:00:28.399
It starts with a call.

00:00:28.399 --> 00:00:33.050
Anitha's father has had a fever for three days, and the front desk is busy.

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:35.035
So our AI receptionist picks up.

00:00:35.835 --> 00:00:41.222
Namaskara, Setu26 Synthetic Hospital. How can I help?

00:00:41.572 --> 00:00:49.349
My father's had a fever for three days. Can we see a general medicine doctor tomorrow morning?

00:00:49.699 --> 00:00:57.316
Tomorrow morning Dr Kavitha Rao has 9:30 and 10:30. Which would you like?

00:00:57.666 --> 00:01:00.483
10:30 works. The name is Shankar Prabhu.

00:01:00.833 --> 00:01:06.113
Shankar Prabhu, tomorrow at 10:30 with Dr Kavitha Rao. Shall I book it?

00:01:06.463 --> 00:01:07.504
Yes, please.

00:01:07.854 --> 00:01:11.400
Done, it's booked. Please come five minutes early.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:18.747
The receptionist only offers times that are really free, and books straight into the doctor's schedule.

00:01:18.747 --> 00:01:22.932
Anything it can't handle goes to your staff as a callback, with the reason.

00:01:23.832 --> 00:01:26.377
And if a caller describes an emergency,

00:01:26.377 --> 00:01:31.597
it stops booking and gives your hospital's emergency instructions straight away.

00:01:32.497 --> 00:01:36.496
At the desk, every call is there, with a transcript and a summary.

00:01:36.496 --> 00:01:38.799
The booking's already on the calendar.

00:01:39.699 --> 00:01:43.720
When Mr Prabhu arrives, the receptionist looks up Anitha's number.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:49.387
His record already exists from the call, so Setu26 finds it instead of registering him twice.

00:01:50.287 --> 00:01:53.117
He keeps one patient ID for every visit.

00:01:53.117 --> 00:02:00.264
Anitha adds her father to her Swasthya app, and the desk connects his profile to his hospital record.

00:02:01.164 --> 00:02:05.600
The front desk sees only what it needs, never the doctors' clinical notes.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:10.137
In the consulting room, the doctor starts a visit and writes her notes.

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:18.145
When she signs, the note is sealed.

00:02:18.145 --> 00:02:21.611
It can be corrected with an addendum, but never quietly changed.

00:02:22.511 --> 00:02:26.686
The prescription goes straight to the family's phone, in the Swasthya app.

00:02:33.161 --> 00:02:35.971
The doctor decides to admit him for observation.

00:02:36.871 --> 00:02:43.066
Reception sees every ward and every bed, floor by floor: free, occupied, or being cleaned.

00:02:43.966 --> 00:02:48.496
They pick a free bed, and he's admitted, with his own admission number.

00:02:48.496 --> 00:02:50.857
One bed can never go to two patients.

00:02:52.566 --> 00:02:55.825
On the ward, nurses work from the Setu26 Staff app.

00:02:56.725 --> 00:03:00.283
They check in for their shift, see their patients bed by bed,

00:03:00.283 --> 00:03:03.257
and record vitals and a bedside note in a few taps.

00:03:10.225 --> 00:03:12.361
Even without a signal, nothing's lost.

00:03:12.361 --> 00:03:18.377
The note goes through as soon as the phone's back online, and the doctor sees it on the web within seconds.

00:03:24.925 --> 00:03:31.422
Nursing tasks, like four-hourly vitals, come up on time, and anything overdue is escalated.

00:03:31.422 --> 00:03:34.635
Medicines wait for a doctor's approval first.

00:03:35.535 --> 00:03:41.317
At the end of the shift, the handover goes to the next nurse, and it's closed only when she's received it.

00:03:46.335 --> 00:03:49.076
Two days later, Mr Prabhu is ready to go home.

00:03:50.835 --> 00:03:55.567
The doctor writes the discharge summary, signs it, and sends it to the family's app.

00:03:55.567 --> 00:03:58.496
It can be printed on the hospital's letterhead, too.

00:04:02.135 --> 00:04:04.555
His bed goes straight to housekeeping.

00:04:04.555 --> 00:04:09.776
It's cleaned, and checked by a second person, before the next patient can have it.

00:04:16.335 --> 00:04:22.333
And for the owner, there's Hospital today: beds by ward, admissions and discharges,

00:04:22.333 --> 00:04:27.031
overdue tasks, beds to clean, and who's on duty, all at a glance.

00:04:29.635 --> 00:04:31.197
For patients, there's Swasthya.

00:04:32.885 --> 00:04:38.215
Families book and track visits, get reports, prescriptions and discharge summaries,

00:04:38.215 --> 00:04:44.188
set medicine reminders, and share older reports with the hospital, only the ones they choose.

00:04:45.088 --> 00:04:47.634
If Anitha turns it on, and only then,

00:04:47.634 --> 00:04:52.932
Swasthya explains the discharge summary in simple words, in her own language.

00:04:52.932 --> 00:04:55.547
It explains; the doctor still decides.

00:04:56.447 --> 00:05:04.711
Phone, front desk, wards and the patient's own phone, on one schedule and one patient record.

00:05:04.711 --> 00:05:05.955
That's Setu26.

00:05:07.455 --> 00:05:13.097
We're inviting a few hospitals to join our founding pilot. Book a demo on our website.
